IMPLEMENTATION_POLICY
    AUTHORITY = Founder, conversa de 2026-10-20
    LANGUAGES = Rust + C + Python
    APPLIES_TO = compiler/runtime/tools/tests/build-support
    CANONICAL_VERSION_REFERENCES = toolchains.json + .python-version

PREFERRED_RESPONSIBILITIES
    Rust = estrutura, frontend, representações, validação, integração e execução
    C = baixo nível, ABI, interação com máquina/runtime quando trouxer benefício
    Python = ferramentas, geração, análise de dados e automação
    responsibilities = FLEXIBLE_NOT_EXCLUSIVE
    choose language from concrete problem, evidence, boundary cost and maintenance
    no duplicate implementation solely to occupy a language
    no requirement to use all three in every feature
    changing a component's language must preserve its accepted semantic contract

COMPOSITION
    Rust + C may combine through a documented C ABI
    Python may orchestrate programs through explicit arguments and exit/result contracts
    Python-generated sources must be deterministic and inspectable when generation is used
    Python embedded in runtime is not assumed or required by this decision
    C does not replace the assembler; emitted/inline assembly is target-specific
    Bash launchers remain thin environment glue, not a fourth product implementation core

DEPENDENCIES
    third-party Rust crates = FORBIDDEN
    third-party Python packages = FORBIDDEN
    third-party C libraries = FORBIDDEN
    development/test/build language packages = SAME_RULE
    vendoring third-party code to evade this rule = FORBIDDEN
    own repository modules and own workspace crates = ALLOWED
    standard language libraries = ALLOWED
    host compiler/interpreter/linker/assembler/Git/shell = DECLARED_ENVIRONMENT_TOOLS
    OS APIs and platform standard C library = DECLARE_WHEN_USED
    transitive implementation dependencies of the toolchain are not claimed absent
    CI checkout/setup actions are infrastructure, not product language libraries
    any additional environment tool must name purpose, version and execution context
    no silent download or package installation by product build/test commands

VERSIONS
    use maintained stable releases, no beta/RC/nightly/development snapshot as baseline
    prefer current corrective/security patches within accepted stable series
    pin reference versions for reproducible validation
    pinned != permanently exempt from security maintenance
    before adoption/update, verify official release/support information and relevant advisories
    upgrade references explicitly and validate affected consumers before claiming support
    record actual rustc/cargo, compiler/linker, Python and target versions in native evidence
    C standard version != C compiler version
    C23 selected explicitly; GCC default dialect must not decide language semantics
    compiler extensions/inline assembly require explicit target contract
    maintained distribution backports must retain package/advisory provenance
    version number alone does not prove absence of vulnerabilities

REFERENCE_SELECTION
    Rust = 1.99.1 stable corrective release
    C = ISO C23 with GCC 16.2 corrective release as reference compiler
    Python = CPython 3.14.8 maintenance/security release
    Python 3.15.0 = newer stable observed, not rejected as unsafe
    Python 3.14.8 choice = conservative maintained-series baseline, not a security ranking
    official evidence = sources in toolchains.json
    compiler/runtime reference execution = NOT_YET_VALIDATED
    Python CI must select exact .python-version and report actual interpreter

BOUNDARY_CONTRACT
    each actual Rust/C interface must define:
        public types, layout/alignment and calling convention
        pointer validity, lengths, aliasing and nullability
        allocation owner, lifetime and matching release function
        error representation, callbacks and thread/reentrancy restrictions
        panic/unwind behavior; no unwinding across an unprepared foreign boundary
        target/architecture constraints and supported compiler options
    each actual Python/process interface must define:
        arguments, cwd/environment, input/output format and version
        exit codes, timeout/interruption behavior and effects on retry
    use fixed-width fields or explicitly target-sized fields where ABI requires them
    validate lengths/ranges before unsafe access; no implicit cross-language ownership
    unsafe code stays localized and explains the invariants its callers must meet
    contract has one implementation authority; other languages consume it

EVIDENCE
    verify real callers and callees together for each imported interface
    cover failure and boundary cases relevant to the obligation
    sanitizers/compiler diagnostics when useful and supported are test tooling
    observations from one target do not prove all architectures
    not every test must traverse all languages
    Trama remains code location only, without a dedicated test suite

LIMIT
    three languages expand implementation choices
    three languages do not prove completeness, memory safety or absence of gaps
    fewer gaps require explicit ownership, integration and observable behavior

CURRENT_STATE
    Python tooling = IMPLEMENTED
    Rust compiler/core = NOT_IMPORTED
    C components = NOT_IMPLEMENTED
    foreign-function interfaces = NOT_IMPLEMENTED
